xnu 0355: flush the root before the reset

The reset paths stop at the SoC: the BCB points the bootloader and the PMIC goes
down, and nothing writes back the root. The root is HFS+ without a journal
(NOTES.md), so the allocation bitmap and the volume header are exactly what the
reset drops and exactly what the next boot needs: its swap-file truncation then
frees blocks the bitmap still calls free -- 13 ranges in one boot, measured
2026-11-18, `hfs: BlockMarkFreeInternal() trying to free unallocated blocks`
followed by `hfs: error truncating data fork!` -- and after a few resets the
volume is far enough from consistent that `hfs_mark_inconsistent` floods the ring
and the compositor stops presenting with `MONDRIAN FB: panel 0/60 sampled ticks
changed`.

`sync(2)` alone is not the flush: it asks each mount for MNT_NOWAIT, which starts
the writes and lets the reset catch them in flight. This walks the mounts and
asks for MNT_WAIT, on both reset paths (`reboot` from the ring, which goes to the
bootloader, and `poweroff`, which stops the SoC).

What to measure: with this patch, `reboot` on the ring must print
"MONDRIAN: flushing the root before the reset to fastboot" and "root flushed",
and the next boot must start with the mark count near zero instead of climbing
into the thousands.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 #include <sys/proc.h>
 #include <sys/systm.h>
 #include <sys/uio.h>
+#include <sys/mount_internal.h>
 #include <sys/namei.h>
 #include <sys/vnode_internal.h>
 #include <machine/atomic.h>
@@ -1549,12 +1550,49 @@
  * reset order goes to the bootloader, neither of which leaves the phone off.
  * Flush the ring, let the gadget go (0307) and ask PSCI for SYSTEM_OFF; the
  * boot control block is not touched, so the next start is a normal boot.
+ *//*
+ * Flush the writable filesystems before a reset.
+ *
+ * The reset paths below stop at the SoC: the BCB points the bootloader and the
+ * PMIC goes down, nothing tells the root to write back what it has in memory.
+ * The root is HFS+ without a journal, so the allocation bitmap and the volume
+ * header are exactly what the next boot needs and exactly what the reset drops:
+ * its swap-file truncation then frees blocks the bitmap calls free (13 ranges
+ * in one boot, measured), and each such boot leaves the volume further from
+ * usable until the compositor stops presenting.
+ *
+ * `sync(2)` is not enough on its own: it asks the mount for MNT_NOWAIT, so the
+ * writes are started and the reset can catch them in flight. This asks for
+ * MNT_WAIT, which is what the volume needs before the power goes away.
  */
+static int
+mondrian_sync_callback(mount_t mp, void *arg)
+{
+	vfs_context_t ctx = vfs_context_kernel();
+
+	(void)arg;
+	if ((mp->mnt_flag & MNT_RDONLY) == 0 && mp->mnt_op != NULL &&
+	    mp->mnt_op->vfs_sync != NULL) {
+		(void)mp->mnt_op->vfs_sync(mp, MNT_WAIT, ctx);
+	}
+	return VFS_RETURNED;
+}
+
+static void
+mondrian_sync_before_reset(const char *why)
+{
+	printf("MONDRIAN: flushing the root before %s\n", why);
+	(void)vfs_iterate(0, mondrian_sync_callback, NULL);
+	printf("MONDRIAN: root flushed\n");
+}
+
+
 void
 mondrian_poweroff(void)
 {
 	extern void mondrian_usb_stop(void);
 
+	mondrian_sync_before_reset("power off");
 	mondrian_log_flush_now();
 	mondrian_usb_stop();
 	mondrian_log_flush_now();
@@ -1602,6 +1640,7 @@
 {
 	extern void mondrian_usb_stop(void);
 
+	mondrian_sync_before_reset("the reset to fastboot");
 	mondrian_log_flush_now();
 	/* The gadget must be idle before the SoC reset (0307). */
 	mondrian_usb_stop();
